<Project>
    <!--
        Framework-family manifest: the Assimalign.Cohesion.App hosting kernel
        and the defaults every framework producer shares. App.<Area>
        frameworks add each resource area's surface; each lists its own
        members in resources/<Area>/Assimalign.Cohesion.<Area>.Runtime/
        Directory.Build.props (see the end of this file).

        Every framework producer imports this file from its project body:

          1. Every Runtime producer - libraries/App/Assimalign.Cohesion.App.Runtime
             and resources/<Area>/Assimalign.Cohesion.<Area>.Runtime (src/...csproj).
             It turns the framework's members into <CohesionProjectReference>
             items so each library is built and CopyLocal'd into the Runtime
             project's bin folder.

          2. Every Refs producer - libraries/App/Assimalign.Cohesion.App.Refs
             and resources/<Area>/Assimalign.Cohesion.<Area>.Refs (src/...csproj)
             (and indirectly, Assimalign.Cohesion.App.targets) drives
             FrameworkList.xml and RuntimeList.xml generation - no DLL
             inspection needed.

        The App ItemGroup below is conditioned on $(CohesionFrameworkName),
        which every Refs/Runtime csproj sets in its PropertyGroup BEFORE
        importing this file, so App's items exist only in App's producers.
        Property-based conditions on top-level ItemGroups are MSBuild-legal
        (item-metadata-based conditions are not). An area's list needs no
        condition: its Directory.Build.props is read only by that area's two
        producers.

        The umbrella assembly per framework is included so the manifest lists
        it; the corresponding Runtime csproj skips it when generating project
        references (a project cannot reference itself).

        SDK policy:
            Sdk          -> no implicit framework (library SDK)
            Sdk.<Area>   -> App + App.<Area> (resource SDK)
            Sdk.Gateway  -> no framework unless in-process composition is active
        CohesionAutoIncludeAppFramework=false suppresses both resource-SDK
        references. The base SDK still registers every framework so direct
        consumers can add an explicit FrameworkReference.

        App is derived, not curated assembly-by-assembly. The
        @(CohesionAppKernelRoot) list below is its only policy input: the host
        and siblings, Connections, host-composed configuration providers,
        console logging, telemetry, dependency injection, and the physical file system. The
        Runtime project references only those roots; App.targets computes their
        transitive Assimalign.Cohesion project-reference closure for the Ref and
        Runtime manifests, then adds the App umbrella assembly. Framework tests
        compare that effective set with the repository project graph.

        ================================================================
        Public vs private assemblies
        ================================================================
        Two parallel item types are recognized per framework:

          @(CohesionFrameworkAssembly)         - PUBLIC. Listed on the Ref
                                                 pack's FrameworkList.xml AND
                                                 shipped in the Runtime pack.
                                                 Visible to consumers at
                                                 compile time (IntelliSense
                                                 sees these types).

          @(CohesionFrameworkPrivateAssembly)  - PRIVATE. NOT listed on the
                                                 Ref pack. Shipped in the
                                                 Runtime pack only, and
                                                 enumerated in RuntimeList.xml
                                                 so the host resolves them.

        Use the private bucket when a framework needs another framework's
        library as an *implementation detail* at runtime but should not
        surface that library to consumers at compile time. The canonical
        example: App.Database needs Assimalign.Cohesion.Web internally to
        host its database server, but a developer using Sdk.Database should
        see only database types in IntelliSense - never web types.

        The library that needs the cross-resource type takes a
        CohesionPrivateProjectReference on the other library inside the repo,
        and keeps every cross-resource use `internal`. The private-assembly
        list is what hides that detail in the shipped artifact.
    -->

    <!--
        Framework producer defaults. Every Refs/Runtime producer imports this
        file from its project body, so these apply to the producers and nothing
        else, wherever they live (libraries/App/ or resources/<Area>/). They
        were frameworks/Directory.Build.props until the producers moved beside
        their areas.

        TargetFramework follows $(TargetFrameworkForFrameworks), not the TFM
        of the libraries/ or resources/ tree a producer sits in.

        Microsoft.NET.Sdk defaults VersionPrefix to 1.0.0, which would land a
        framework .nupkg at 1.0.0 instead of $(CohesionVersion). Setting the
        prefix/suffix split here - after the global props define
        $(CohesionVersionPrefix) / $(CohesionVersionSuffix), before
        Microsoft.NET.Sdk.targets composes Version - keeps every framework
        package on $(CohesionVersion). Use the split, not $(CohesionVersion)
        directly: a prerelease semver fed to [assembly: AssemblyVersion]
        fails with CS7034.

        Description: build/Targets/Build.Branding.props words it from the
        project name, and a producer's project name follows its area
        (Assimalign.Cohesion.Web.Refs) while everything a consumer sees
        follows the framework. Naming the producer <framework>.Refs /
        <framework>.Runtime keeps the published text unchanged.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>$(TargetFrameworkForFrameworks)</TargetFramework>
        <VersionPrefix>$(CohesionVersionPrefix)</VersionPrefix>
        <VersionSuffix Condition="'$(CohesionVersionSuffix)' != ''">$(CohesionVersionSuffix)</VersionSuffix>
        <_CohesionFrameworkProducerName Condition="'$(CohesionFrameworkKind)' == 'Ref'">$(CohesionFrameworkName).Refs</_CohesionFrameworkProducerName>
        <_CohesionFrameworkProducerName Condition="'$(CohesionFrameworkKind)' == 'Runtime'">$(CohesionFrameworkName).Runtime</_CohesionFrameworkProducerName>
        <Description Condition="'$(_CohesionFrameworkProducerName)' != ''">$(Description.Replace('$(MSBuildProjectName)', '$(_CohesionFrameworkProducerName)'))</Description>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- ================================================================
         Assimalign.Cohesion.App: hosting kernel. Resource-area SDKs include it;
         direct base-SDK consumers opt in explicitly.
         ================================================================ -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(CohesionFrameworkName)' == 'Assimalign.Cohesion.App'">
        <!-- This is the sole policy list for the hosting kernel. App.targets derives
             every transitive Assimalign.Cohesion dependency from these roots. -->
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Hosting" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Hosting.Health" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Hosting.Resources" />
        <!-- Generated resource accessors and every area hosting module depend on Connections. -->
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Connections" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Hosting.Telemetry" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Configuration.CommandLine" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Configuration.EnvironmentVariables" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Configuration.FileSystem" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Configuration.Json" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.Logging.Console" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.OpenTelemetry" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.DependencyInjection" />
        <CohesionAppKernelRoot Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.FileSystem.Physical" />

        <CohesionFrameworkAssembly Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.App" />

        <!--
            Roslyn analyzers / source generators shipped INSIDE this framework's
            Ref pack at analyzers/dotnet/cs/. SDK-path consumers (i.e. anyone
            using <Project Sdk="Assimalign.Cohesion.Sdk">) auto-pick these up
            via the .NET SDK's ResolveTargetingPackAssets, the same way a
            direct <PackageReference> consumer auto-picks up the analyzer
            bundled inside the owning library's own .nupkg. By-name lookup
            against analyzers/**/*.csproj.

            The general component-integration generator is required because
            kernel assemblies carry ComponentIntegration declarations. Feature
            analyzers travel with their ordinary NuGet packages or area packs.
        -->
        <!-- Projects [assembly: ComponentIntegration] declarations into consuming-compilation extension verbs. -->
        <CohesionFrameworkAnalyzer Include="Assimalign.Cohesion.SourceGeneration.ComponentModel" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!-- ================================================================
         Resource-domain frameworks. Each corresponds to a folder under
         resources/, whose Assimalign.Cohesion.<Area>.Refs and .Runtime
         projects produce it. Its public/private inventory lives with its
         Runtime producer, in
         resources/<Area>/Assimalign.Cohesion.<Area>.Runtime/Directory.Build.props,
         which the sibling Refs producer imports as its own Directory.Build.props.
         The inventory covers the transitive Assimalign.Cohesion closure of the
         area's shipped surface; the framework tests enforce that coverage
         against the project graph.
         ================================================================ -->
</Project>
